more people  you particularly see this again in  nonpartisan or primary elections  you will see you know regularly the the  winners say gosh  or losers too you know this was really  interesting i mean i just  threw out the playbook that you just  talked to your base and swing voters and  and talk to everybody and just look for  a connection right which is what you  want i think  and that just comes from the voter knows  that they now have a second or third  choice that could matter and so they  need to think  more about candidates and the candidate  knows that and that ultimately as a  representative just has learned a lot  more about the people there  that they're going to represent not the  be all and end all but a clearly just  sort of  uh step forward the multi-member  district particularly you know with  with a proportional system um and these  are these candidate-based american-style  kind of forms of proportional voting  we've had some examples of them but but  when you  the the the two really kind of clears  differences  are that every representative will  will share constituents with someone  from a different party  so you have a different reason when  you're sort of serving your constituency  to talk with people of another party  because you actually  share representative you know you share  constituents and you're  when the parties retreat to their  caucuses they kind of bring the whole  country the whole state in  in into the room and the interests of  cities are part of republican caucuses  and of rural areas are part of  democratic caucuses  and so the attentiveness of both  caucuses  major parties at least to to be thinking  kind of thinking about the whole country  changes  and of course the rank choice feature  kind of creates different  different opportunities to be rewarded  for getting for for sort of solving  problems  for for at least some representatives  that's what they will be in office for  and they can get reelected to do that  so thanks to all the panelists for uh  offering  fresh ideas and insights on a perennial  question how do we get a better  representation and hopefully better  outcomes  a reminder just to visit the isps  website  isps.yale.edu  keep up with events you can also join  our mailing lists  to receive a monthly newsletter um uh  event information on future events  uh or follow us on twitter and facebook  and lastly just a big thank you to the  organizers pam green  uh pamela monica tori bilsky and lamora  peer  of course to all the people in the  audience who have such great questions  and participated so much  so thank you very much and we'll see you  the next event
 

